1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,630
For Ethereum, if you want
to change the protocol,

2
00:00:02,630 --> 00:00:05,430
what had been done in the past
is called a hard fork.

3
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:06,790
You end up with two versions, right?

4
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:08,870
There is the old version,
there is the new version.

5
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:10,550
In Redbelly it's slightly different,

6
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:13,730
in the sense that everyone
who is running the protocol

7
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,960
knows from day one what they have accepted,

8
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,480
which is essentially the code base.
It's like the constitution, right?

9
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:21,240
They all agreed to the code base,

10
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:25,160
which says that if at some point
the governance decides to change

11
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:27,710
the version of the protocol,
then the protocol will change.

12
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:30,180
Whether you like it or not.

13
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:32,740
So you run your node, the code executes,

14
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:35,460
and the code itself says, oh,

15
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:38,300
the governance tells us
to change the version.

16
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,250
I'm not gonna wait for the human user

17
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:43,570
to instruct me to change the version.
